      "findings": [
        {
          "gate": "reasoning_validity",
          "severity": "minor",
          "source": "reviewer",
          "detail": "D1 [verdict 'Terms to ask for' item 1; practicalImplications last item; counterarguments third response] The fallback rule for a refused carve-out cites only the 44.3% direct floor (K19), which holds only if finance's EUR 7.0M excludes the existing base. If the EUR 7.0M includes the base, the draft's own K20 puts direct's 3-year floor at 67.4%, so 'prefer direct above 44.3%' would recommend a direct team that loses money against the status quo between 44.3% and 67.4%. Draft 3 rewrote these sentences to add the Y4+ caveat but kept the single threshold. The rule should read 'above 44.3% (K19), or 67.4% if the EUR 7.0M includes the base (K20)'."
        },
        {
          "gate": "reasoning_validity",
          "severity": "minor",
          "source": "reviewer",
          "detail": "D2 [verdict paragraph 'Why C6 does not reverse the ranking'; K38; CL18; furtherResearch item 5] The recomputed K38 (EUR 2.74M/yr) checks out: (0.495 - 0.16 + 1.033)/0.5 = 2.737. But it is still calculated at the 35% discount. The verdict now uses K38 to explain why C6 does not reverse the ranking 'when all three conditions hold', and one of those conditions is a 25-30% discount. At 30%, Meridian's Y3 run-rate contribution rises to 3.3 x 0.20 = 0.66, so the post-term revenue direct needs becomes (0.66 - 0.16 + 1.033)/0.5, about EUR 3.07M/yr. So the 2.74M figure understates what direct must reach under the scenario where the draft prefers Meridian. The post-term threshold should be stated at the recommended discount."
        }
      ]
    },
